# baseline.sha256 - backward-compatibility baseline for the legacy default output
# of index.js. This file is the mechanism behind acceptance gate G6: the legacy
# standard output of the JavaScript application must hash to the value recorded on
# the last line of this file. It exists so that a regression in the default
# invocation fails loudly and mechanically, rather than depending on a reviewer
# noticing that five lines of "12" turned into four.
#
# WHAT IS HASHED
#   The bytes written to STANDARD OUTPUT by `node index.js` invoked with no
#   arguments: 15 bytes, "12\n" repeated five times - the result of add(5, 7)
#   printed by the five original console.log statements - with exit status 0.
#
# WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HASHED
#   - The source text of index.js. The contract is the observable behaviour, not
#     the implementation, so a legitimate edit to index.js must never move this
#     value. Hashing the source would break on every edit and would prove nothing
#     about behaviour.
#   - Standard error. The --serve and --probe diagnostics are written to stderr
#     precisely so that they can never pollute the hashed stream, so capture
#     stdout alone (`2>/dev/null`, or a separate redirect) and never `2>&1`.
#   - The Python and Java default outputs. Those are asserted directly in the
#     workflow ("Hello Lakshya" and "Test") and are deliberately not recorded here.
#
# PRODUCING RUNTIME: Node.js 24.18.0 (pinned in .nvmrc)
#   Recording the producing runtime is mandatory provenance: a baseline committed
#   without its runtime version creates a check that can fail for the wrong
#   reason. The value is a SHA-256 over a fixed 15-byte sequence, so it is
#   content-based and therefore runtime-independent - it was re-verified
#   byte-identical on the runtime installed in this environment, v22.23.1, which
#   satisfies the engines.node floor of >=22.12.0 declared in package.json.
#
# VERIFY (must print the hash recorded on the last line):
#   node index.js | sha256sum
#
# GATE G6 (fails closed - non-zero exit on mismatch, never retried):
#   expected="$(grep -v '^#' baseline.sha256 | awk 'NF{print $1; exit}')"
#   actual="$(node index.js | sha256sum | awk '{print $1}')"
#   test "$expected" = "$actual" && echo "G6 PASS" || { echo "G6 FAIL"; exit 1; }
#
# ON MISMATCH
#   Fix forward in index.js. A mismatch means the default branch of the entry
#   point no longer produces the bytes it has always produced, which is a
#   backward-compatibility failure. Never regenerate this hash to make the gate
#   pass: the recorded value IS the contract, and rewriting it would silently
#   discard the behaviour the gate exists to protect. In particular, the five
#   identical writes are the output contract - they are not to be de-duplicated
#   or collapsed into a loop.
#
# FORMAT
#   One sha256sum-style record: 64 lowercase hexadecimal digits, two spaces, then
#   a label. The label names a byte stream rather than a file on disk, so
#   `sha256sum -c baseline.sha256` is not the supported check and is expected to
#   report a missing file; use the extraction command above instead. Every other
#   line here is a comment beginning with '#', there are no blank lines, and the
#   record is always the last line, so any `grep -v '^#'` or `awk '!/^#/'`
#   extraction yields it.
56990d15cb2da6455eec9f9d56432fdeb611e39388f4764725129962343e5fc4  legacy-index-js-stdout
